import{_ as n,P as a,d as i,e as o,y as s,q as r,f as c}from"./chunks/framework.D7CAgLCq.js";const v=JSON.parse(`{"title":"D.20 State Capacity, Policy Implementation, and Governance","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"D.20 State Capacity, Policy Implementation, and Governance","xkChars":0,"xkWords":458,"xkLessons":{"D.20":{"title":"D.20 State Capacity, Policy Implementation, and Governance","lead":"Before asking whether governance has capacity, ask for which task, at which stage, for which population, and within what period. Success inside a procedure cannot substitute for coverage of the entire eligible population.","explanation":["Chinese source location: lines 4925–4940, original site location /theory/appendix-d/d-20#section-d-20. See the theory-v2 source for the current complete text; the historical research manuscript and excerpts remain available to download and consult in their original edition.","Brief quotation, translated from Chinese: ‘State capacity should be specified in terms of concrete tasks, levels, and organizational resources.’ State formation, fiscal and military mobilization, administrative autonomy, embeddedness, legitimacy, identification, and public services are distinct processes. Strong implementation in one area does not prove strength elsewhere. Nor can observed compliance be attributed solely to identification: dependence, penalties, or routine practices may also be explanations requiring investigation. [T-main]","The policy chain distinguishes at least problem entry onto the agenda, rulemaking, eligibility definition, budget delivery, frontline implementation, notification and application, determination and payment, actual use, and appeal and correction. Completion of one stage may condition the next, but does not substitute for its actual realization; for example, funds reaching an institution do not mean an applicant can use a service. [T-main]","Denominators must be explicit at every stage. Eligible people, notified people, applicants, approved applicants, recipients, and those with sustained usable access each have their own sets and time periods. The approval rate can truly increase while overall receipt coverage remains low. If eligibility is counted by household and receipt by individual, mapping and duplication must be explained; the two counts cannot simply be divided. [T-main]","Frontline discretion can compensate for poorly fitting rules or create exclusion. Examine conflicting tasks, resources, actual choices, and oversight rather than prejudging every workaround as dereliction or innovation. Polycentric governance also means more than opening additional institutions: each must possess real powers, resources, and coordination. If appointments, funding, sampling, implementation, and review all pass through the same controlling gate, the number of institutions does not prove independence. [T-main]","The usefulness of local knowledge does not mean locally powerful people represent tenants, minorities, nonparticipants, and future users. Connections to higher authorities can provide resources and remedies, but can also control objectives and individual cases. Autonomy must specify relative to whom and in which matter: freedom from party control, discretion within administrative rules, and local or user powers to change rules do not lie on the same axis. [T-main]","The random-inspection example in the source is a teaching scenario. More complete records can constitute a local improvement in oversight without proving faster remedies for ordinary applicants. Temporary assistance can also genuinely improve living conditions while long-term fiscal continuity and freedom of choice remain unresolved. Institutional externalities should identify which decision-making calculation omits which costs. An evidenced harm can be described before a final determination of legal liability. [T-main]","Real-world reference: Recommendation 13.1 of Australia's Royal Commission into the Robodebt Scheme report of July 7, 2023 calls for consultation with frontline staff in designing and implementing new programs; Recommendation 13.2 calls for management to review and respond to their feedback. The additional observation here is that ‘receiving frontline input’ differs from ‘management responding’ within the policy implementation chain. This does not reuse the case as one about legal authorization. Only the recommendation text is checked here; it does not claim that the recommendations were implemented or that applicants received remedies. [P-ROBO]","§§7.3.7–7.3.8 provide interfaces for feedback and the conditional potential field; Parts X–XI trace layers, channels, and events; Part XIV examines normative choice and authorization; and §15.3.5 connects to the institutional module. These connections do not reduce state capacity to a single HV score: specific tasks still require domain evidence. [T-main]","Research navigation: 20.01, state formation and the fiscal-military state (Tilly and others), asks how resource mobilization forms; 20.02, legitimate domination and identification (Weber and others), distinguishes grounds of compliance; 20.03, state capacity, autonomy, and embeddedness (Mann, Evans, Fukuyama, and others), requires specified tasks and relationships; 20.04, street-level bureaucracy (Lipsky), concerns frontline discretion; and 20.05, polycentricity and the commons (Ostrom), concerns real powers and coordination. This lesson explains the book's entry points without claiming to have read these scholars' complete works. [T-main]","20.06, public policy implementation and feedback, connects to the entire policy chain; 20.07, central–local relations, examines scale, resources, and control; 20.08, corruption, capture, and principal–agent relations, asks who appoints, pays, samples, and reviews; 20.09, comparisons of democratic, authoritarian, bureaucratic, and technocratic governance, must hold tasks and populations constant rather than produce overall rankings; and 20.10, governance failure, fragility, and reconstruction, separates functional interruption from subsequent continuation. One failure cannot establish that all state capacities have fallen to zero. [T-main]"],"argumentSteps":["Fix the task, level, target population, and period.","List the state of each policy-chain stage and its actual evidence; do not use upstream success to fill downstream gaps.","Specify denominators, household-to-person mappings, and duration.","Examine frontline discretion and the structure of oversight and control; distinguish nominal polycentricity from actual powers.","Retain local improvements and omitted harms; check effects, legality, and normative choice separately."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Approval rate","leftText":"Number approved / number applying.","rightTitle":"Population coverage","rightText":"The proportion of the eligible population reached and the continuity of usable access."},{"leftTitle":"Complete records","leftText":"An oversight or recordkeeping endpoint.","rightTitle":"Actual remedy","rightText":"Decisions, resources, and living outcomes require separate evidence."},{"leftTitle":"Many institutions","leftText":"The number of organizations.","rightTitle":"Polycentric governance","rightText":"Real decentralization of powers and relationships of coordination."},{"leftTitle":"Autonomy","leftText":"Room for action relative to a specified actor and matter.","rightTitle":"Overall strength or weakness","rightText":"Cannot replace analysis along individual axes."}],"boundaries":["Teaching counterexample from the source: inspections improve records while remedies in individual cases remain slow.","Teaching counterexample: a high approval rate among applicants is taken to mean that eligible nonapplicants are also covered.","Source boundary: connections to higher authorities can bring both resources and control; they cannot be assigned a uniformly positive or negative meaning."],"questions":["Does a high approval rate show that everyone receives benefits?","Why does local participation not automatically represent everyone?","Does the existence of a commission recommendation mean it has been implemented?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-D-20/figures/d20-chain.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"In 2023, the Robodebt commission called for frontline feedback to connect to management responses","href":"https://robodebt.royalcommission.gov.au/publications/report"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"In 2023, the Robodebt commission called for frontline feedback to connect to management responses","text":"Recommendation 13.1 of the July 7, 2023 report calls for frontline consultation during design and implementation; Recommendation 13.2 calls for management to review and respond to feedback.","sourceLabel":"List of recommendations in the Royal Commission into the Robodebt Scheme: Report","sourceUrl":"https://robodebt.royalcommission.gov.au/publications/report","sourceLocator":"Report page dated 2023-07-07; Chapter 13, Recommendation 13.1, Consultation process, and Recommendation 13.2, Feedback processes; correction notice dated 2023-07-11","boundary":"The recommendation text alone does not prove implementation, faster remedies in individual cases, or overall state governance capacity. This lesson examines an additional observation point in the implementation chain.","href":"https://robodebt.royalcommission.gov.au/publications/report"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"D.20","start_line":5016,"end_line":5031,"startLine":5016,"endLine":5031}],"outputId":"section-d-20","sourceHash":"51795bb7988f2669a324a52666c320cfba1382f597f7b5c6a1431844572da1fb","teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: do not mix four different ratios","text":"In an artificial eligible population of 100 people, 40 apply, 30 are approved, and 24 actually receive benefits. The approval rate is 30/40 = 75%; receipt among approved applicants is 24/30 = 80%; receipt among all applicants is 24/40 = 60%; and receipt coverage of the eligible population is 24/100 = 24%. All four fractions are correct, but they answer different questions. Without records for subsequent months, this cannot be called sustained 24% coverage.","assumptions":"A fictional example using the same window, individual counting units, nested populations, and no duplication.","boundary":"This represents no real policy effect; household units or different windows require recalculation."},{"title":"Teaching construction: a limited conclusion about improved oversight","text":"In an artificial example, incomplete records fall from 10 to 2 after an inspection, but no data on appeal processing times have been obtained. The change in the recordkeeping endpoint can be registered. Attributing it to the inspection still requires a causal comparison, and claiming faster remedies requires corresponding timing and downstream evidence.","assumptions":"The numbers are entirely fictional; the before-and-after difference is not treated directly as a causal effect.","boundary":"Do not erase the observed improvement in records or use it to prove that all governance is effective."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching counterexample from the source: inspections improve records while remedies in individual cases remain slow.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching counterexample: a high approval rate among applicants is taken to mean that eligible nonapplicants are also covered.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Source boundary: connections to higher authorities can bring both resources and control; they cannot be assigned a uniformly positive or negative meaning.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Does a high approval rate show that everyone receives benefits?","answer":"No. Nonapplicants and nonrecipients may be absent from the denominator, and sustained usable access requires a separate check.","basis":"The policy chain."},{"question":"Why does local participation not automatically represent everyone?","answer":"Representative relationships and omitted positions must be examined; the voices of locally powerful people may not cover everyone affected.","basis":"Power and coverage."},{"question":"Does the existence of a commission recommendation mean it has been implemented?","answer":"No. Independent evidence is needed about management changes, implementation, and applicant outcomes.","basis":"The limits of the real-world case."}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-D-20/figures/d20-chain.png","alt":"Agenda setting, rules, eligibility, budgets, implementation, applications, payments, use, and appeals are distinct successive stages; reaching the next is not guaranteed.","caption":"Based on the policy chain in D.20."},{"path":"/reading-assets/S-D-20/figures/d20-denominators.png","alt":"Artificial figures: 100 eligible, 40 applying, 30 approved, and 24 receiving. The four ratios use different denominators.","caption":"A teaching construction, not policy data."},{"path":"/reading-assets/S-D-20/figures/d20-governance.png","alt":"Check discretion, powers, resources, appointments, sampling, and review separately; a recommendation does not equal implementation.","caption":"Based on D.20 and the commission's Recommendations 13.1–13.2."}],"diagramBrief":"Three figures show the policy chain, artificial denominators, and relationships of autonomy and oversight; they do not rank countries by overall capacity.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This unit concerns methodological judgment. Actual sources support only the listed facts; the diagram and teaching construction each retain their own status."},"openQuestions":["A comprehensive comparison of the scholars' original works has not been completed, nor has any overall ranking of state capacity been validated.","The lesson's source record reports that a direct refetch of the official recommendations page timed out. Facts are limited to the recommendation text already checked; no implementation progress is reported."],"taskId":"S-D-20","sources":[{"id":"T-main","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§D.20, historical Chinese research manuscript lines 4837–4852","claim_support":["The full policy chain, denominators, autonomy, teaching examples, and ten thematic entry points."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"P-ROBO","title":"List of recommendations in the Royal Commission into the Robodebt Scheme: Report","url":"https://robodebt.royalcommission.gov.au/publications/report","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"2023-07-07; the page records a correction on 2023-07-11","locator":"Report page dated 2023-07-07; Chapter 13, Recommendation 13.1, Consultation process, and Recommendation 13.2, Feedback processes; correction notice dated 2023-07-11","claim_support":["The commission calls for genuinely consulting frontline staff when designing and implementing new programs, and establishing processes for management to review and respond to feedback."],"limits":["The lesson's source record reports that the recommendations on the official site were read. A direct refetch timed out, but the official page's indexed text still allowed both complete recommendations to be checked. The full 990-page report was not read, and the recommendations are not used to infer implementation or remedial effects."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v2","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2 / 2026-11-16","locator":"D.20 State Capacity, Policy Implementation, and Governance; Chinese source lines 4925–4940; SHA-256 6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-D-20/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-D-20/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-D-20/source/S-D-20.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-D-20/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text and section locations correspond to v2; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"27eba442dfaf0ac0c798bedf9b15b3be3be3d42142b6097d2f8a589dbc455f87","source_sha256":"6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c"}}}},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/theory/appendix-d/d-20.md","filePath":"en/theory/appendix-d/d-20.md"}`),l={name:"en/theory/appendix-d/d-20.md"};function d(p,e,m,h,u,f){const t=a("SectionLesson");return i(),o("div",null,[e[0]||(e[0]=s("",1)),r(t,{section:"D.20"}),e[1]||(e[1]=c("div",{class:"theory-source"},null,-1))])}const y=n(l,[["render",d]]);export{v as __pageData,y as default};
